            "<strong>Oral rehydration solution</strong> is one of the great inventions of medicine: the low-osmolarity formula (sodium 75, glucose 75, potassium 20, chloride 65 and citrate 10 mmol/L — 245 mOsm/L) uses the gut's coupled sodium–glucose transporter, which cholera toxin does not block, to pull water back into the body as fast as the toxin pushes it out. One sachet is dissolved in <strong>one litre</strong> of clean (boiled and cooled, or treated) water — not half a litre to make it ‘stronger’, which makes a hypertonic solution that worsens the diarrhoea — stirred until clear, kept covered and discarded after twenty-four hours, and given with a cup and spoon, small amounts frequently, continuing through vomiting with a ten-minute pause and smaller sips; where no sachet exists, a home solution of six level teaspoons of sugar and half a level teaspoon of salt in a litre of clean water, or salted rice water and soups, hold the line until it arrives. <strong>Zinc</strong> — 10 mg daily under six months, 20 mg from six months, for ten to fourteen days, dissolved on a spoon of breast milk or ORS — shortens the episode, reduces its severity and prevents the next one for two to three months, and is given in every diarrhoea whatever the plan. <strong>Feeding continues</strong>: breastfeeding throughout, and in the older child small frequent energy-dense meals during the illness and one extra meal a day for two weeks afterwards, because the fast that mothers and some nurses impose starves a child who needs to repair his gut. The adult with cholera on Plan C needs the same arithmetic — a 60 kg man needs six litres in the first three hours, and then <strong>ongoing losses</strong> replaced litre for litre as measured in the bucket under his cot — and his potassium, which Ringer's lactate and ORS both contain, is why saline alone is a poorer choice and dextrose alone is dangerous.",
