            "When cases multiply, patients are not scattered through the general wards; a <strong>cholera treatment centre</strong> (or a smaller treatment unit at a health centre) is opened on a site with water, drainage, and distance from kitchens and wards, and it is designed around one idea — <strong>nothing that comes out of a patient reaches anyone's mouth</strong>. The layout is a <strong>one-way flow</strong>: a screening and triage area at the single entrance where every arrival is assessed, classified and recorded; an <strong>observation area</strong> (the ORS corner) for Plan A and B patients and their carers; a <strong>hospitalisation ward</strong> for Plan C patients on <strong>cholera cots</strong> — canvas beds with a hole under the buttocks and a graduated bucket beneath, and a second bucket for vomit, so that losses are measured hourly and added to the fluid plan; a <strong>recovery ward</strong>; and an exit for the discharged, separate from the entrance. Around them sit a neutral zone for staff rest and the pharmacy, a stores and preparation area for ORS and chlorine, a kitchen with controlled food, latrines and showers for patients and separate ones for staff, a waste zone with a pit and an incinerator, a morgue, and a fence with a <strong>footbath</strong> of 0.2 per cent chlorine and a handwashing station at every doorway. Water is needed in volume — about sixty litres per patient per day for drinking, ORS, washing and cleaning — and every drop of it chlorinated.",
