            "A cholera outbreak is investigated in the ordered steps of lesson 1.2 — confirm the diagnosis (culture of the first five to ten cases, then clinical case definitions), confirm that an outbreak exists (one confirmed case is an outbreak in cholera), define a case by person, place and time, find and count cases through active search in the community and all facilities, describe them by person, place and time on a line list, an epidemic curve and a spot map, form a hypothesis (which well, which vendor, which funeral), test it by comparing attack rates and sampling water and food, and then report and review — but <strong>control does not wait</strong>: the moment a plausible source is identified the water is chlorinated, the vendor is stopped and the funeral precautions are announced, while the laboratory works. The control package is <strong>WASH plus case management plus communication plus vaccine</strong>: free chlorine residual in the piped supply raised to 0.5 mg/L or more at the point of use; chlorination of wells and tanker water; <strong>household water treatment</strong> with chlorine tablets (one tablet of the strength supplied per twenty-litre container, thirty minutes before use) or boiling, and covered storage with a ladle rather than a dipped cup; emergency latrines and an end to open defecation, with latrines sited at least thirty metres from and downhill of any well; soap and handwashing stations at every latrine, market and food stall; food cooked thoroughly, eaten hot, covered, and no raw or street-vended cold food; safe burial and funeral guidance through chiefs, imams and pastors; <strong>risk communication</strong> through radio, information vans, schools and community meetings that tells people where the treatment centre and the oral rehydration points are, and that early rehydration saves lives; and the <strong>oral cholera vaccine</strong> — a killed whole-cell vaccine given by mouth, two doses two weeks apart (a single dose in an emergency campaign) to everyone over one year, protecting about two in three for three years — deployed reactively in hotspots and pre-emptively in known high-risk districts, never as a replacement for water and sanitation. The outbreak is declared over after two incubation periods — in practice several weeks — without a new case.",
