            "<strong>Hepatitis A</strong> and <strong>hepatitis E</strong> are the two hepatitis viruses that travel by the faecal–oral route — contaminated water, food washed in it, shellfish, and the hands of an infected food handler — and neither causes chronic infection. Hepatitis A, with an incubation of two to seven weeks, infects almost every Ghanaian child before the age of ten, usually without symptoms; the older the patient the more likely a clinical illness of fever, malaise, anorexia, nausea, right-upper-quadrant pain, dark urine, pale stools and jaundice lasting two to eight weeks, with fulminant hepatitis in fewer than one in a hundred. An inactivated vaccine exists and is given to travellers and to adults with chronic liver disease who have escaped childhood infection. Hepatitis E has an incubation of two to nine weeks and causes the same illness, usually in young adults and often in waterborne outbreaks after floods or in camps — but in <strong>pregnant women in the third trimester</strong> it causes fulminant liver failure in up to one in four, with high maternal and foetal mortality, so a pregnant woman with jaundice is a medical emergency admitted and watched for encephalopathy, bleeding and hypoglycaemia, not a clinic patient told to rest. There is no specific treatment for either: rest, fluids, a light diet, no alcohol, no herbal mixtures, paracetamol only in modest doses, monitoring of the prothrombin time and conscious level, and enteric precautions — a separate toilet, hand hygiene, no food preparation for others until a week after the jaundice appears. In the surveillance system <strong>acute jaundice syndrome</strong> is reported and the first sample is tested for yellow fever, because the diseases cannot be separated at the bedside and one of them demands a vaccination campaign.",
