            "Ghana's sanitation figures are read by the rung: around a fifth of households still practise <strong>open defecation</strong> — more than half in parts of the north — and many more use shared or unimproved facilities, while the <strong>safely managed</strong> rung, where the waste is also treated, remains the exception. <strong>Community-led total sanitation</strong> changes this not by building latrines but by walking a community to its own defecation sites, letting it trace the faeces to its food and water, and supporting its own decision to build with its own materials until it is verified <strong>open-defecation-free</strong>; the nurse at the CHPS compound is the natural facilitator and the one who keeps the community from sliding back when pits collapse in the rains. The <strong>water ladder</strong> runs from surface water through unimproved and limited sources to basic and safely managed supply, and a borehole that nobody maintains slips down it within a season. <strong>Food safety</strong> is regulated through the Food and Drugs Authority and the assemblies' food-handler certificates, which require a medical examination — stool for typhoid carriage and parasites, a chest and skin check — and the environmental health officer's inspection of the stall, and is taught by the nurse through the five keys to safer food. Where all this is done, typhoid, hepatitis A and E, polio, cholera and the worms recede together, because they all walk the same five routes.",
